# VG-1's suppression list, and it holds FOUR FINGERPRINTS THAT ARE ALL ONE
# VALUE. Written by session 254, which is the session that created the finding.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE FINDING IS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `gitleaks`' `generic-api-key` rule fires on a high-entropy string assigned to a
# key whose name contains `token`. Session 254's `affiliate.test.ts` wrote
#
#   click_token: '<a fabricated uuid>'
#
# inside a TEST DOUBLE's return value, and the two session logs that quote the
# pasted HTTP response quote it again. Four sites, one fabricated uuid, no
# credential: `POST /affiliate/links` has no wiring, no vendor and no secret, and
# the value was typed by hand into a fixture.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A SUPPRESSION AND NOT A REPAIR, WHICH IS THE PART THAT NEEDS DEFENDING
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BECAUSE `gitleaks git .` READS COMMITS AND NOT THE WORKING TREE, AND THESE FOUR
# COMMITS ARE IN `main`. Changing the fixture forward, which session 254 also
# did, leaves all four findings exactly where they are, forever. The only repair
# that removes them is rewriting published history on the default branch, and
# that invalidates every checkout and every open branch in the repository to
# suppress a fabricated uuid.
#
# SESSION 255 REACHED THE SAME TWO OPTIONS AND ASSIGNED THIS ONE HERE. Its
# section 10 closes: *"Session 254's branch is where this closes, either by the
# fixture moving out of a `*token*` key or by a `.gitleaksignore` that session
# owns."* Both halves are taken, because after the merge neither is sufficient
# alone.
#
# SESSION 255's OWN PROPOSED WORKFLOW FIX IS NOW DEAD AND THAT IS WORTH SAYING
# HERE. It proposed `--log-opts="HEAD"` on the gitleaks step, on the measurement
# that the findings lived on one unmerged branch while `fetch-depth: 0` made the
# scan read every ref. That was right on the day it was written. `#336` merged,
# so the commits are ancestors of `HEAD` now: re-measured against `main` at
# `a4f1bfc`, both `--log-opts="HEAD"` and `--log-opts="origin/main"` report
# **4 leaks over 341 commits**, identical to the unscoped run. The workflow is
# therefore NOT edited, because the edit no longer changes the result.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS NOT "WEAKENING A GATE TO PASS IT", STATED SO IT CAN BE OVERTURNED
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A fingerprint is `commit:file:rule:startline` and every field is pinned. These
# four entries cannot suppress a fifth finding, cannot suppress the same rule in
# another file, and cannot suppress the same file at another line. The RULE is
# untouched and every future commit is scanned by it exactly as before; what is
# suppressed is four immutable points in a history that cannot change.
#
# THE COMMIT SHAs HERE ARE STABLE IN A WAY THEY WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A DAY AGO,
# which is the objection session 255 raised against writing this file from its
# own branch and it was correct then: a fingerprint naming an unmerged commit
# goes stale silently the moment that branch is rebased. All four of these are
# merged into `main` and can never be rebased away, so the staleness mode that
# objection names does not exist for them.
#
# **THE HONEST COST, WHICH IS REAL:** a future secret committed at exactly one of
# these four (commit, file, line) points would be suppressed. That is impossible
# rather than unlikely, since a commit's content is fixed once written.
#
# ADDING A FIFTH ENTRY HERE IS A DIFFERENT DECISION FROM ADDING THESE FOUR, and a
# session that finds itself wanting one should read this header first: these
# exist because a merge made the repair unavailable, not because a fixture was
# inconvenient to change.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO SHAPES BELOW ARE TWO DIFFERENT SCANS, WHICH IS WHY SIX ENTRIES COVER
# FOUR SITES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ci.yml` runs gitleaks TWICE and the second is not redundant: `gitleaks git .`
# reads commits and `gitleaks dir .` reads the working tree, so a value that
# survives in a checked-out file is found by the second even when the first is
# clean. A `git` fingerprint carries the commit and a `dir` fingerprint does not.
#
# THE TWO `dir` ENTRIES ARE LINE-NUMBERED AND THEREFORE FRAGILE, AND THAT IS
# ACCEPTED RATHER THAN OVERLOOKED. Both name a session log, both logs are
# APPEND-ONLY RECORDS under ADR-118, and appending does not move an earlier line.
# Session 254's own section 9 was appended after these were pinned and both lines
# held. An edit that inserts ABOVE either line turns `CI-05` red with a stale
# fingerprint, which is loud rather than silent and is the failure mode to
# prefer.
#
# NEITHER LOG IS EDITED TO SATISFY THIS SCANNER. Both quote the value inside a
# pasted HTTP response, and rewriting evidence to make a red light green is the
# move this repository exists to refuse. Session 255 may of course change its own
# quote; that entry disappears from here when it does.

